F0_R0_OLD = 'So the per-worker budget is 2.4x smaller than the figure it was derived from,\nand decision 3 below -- "the numbers are measured by a tool, not asserted in a\ncomment" -- is the claim that failed: the tool was measuring a discarded object\nand reporting it as a measurement.\n\n**`WORKER_BYTES` is deliberately left at 900 MB.** The corrected figures say the\nfour-worker budget does not hold; the recorded four-worker tree above says four\nworkers fitted in 2.9 GB with the parent down to 120 MB, which cannot be true if\none worker holds 2.1 GB. Both cannot be right, and one of them has now been\nshown to be a measurement error, so the honest position is that this ADR holds\none good measurement, one bad one withdrawn, and one unexplained. Changing the\nconstant would change the worker count on every machine and in CI on the strength\nof a contradiction rather than a measurement.\n\nSettling it needs one run this machine could not do: a real four-worker\nbuild, which needs about 10 GB free. Two things are already true and do not wait\nfor it. `tools/measure_build_memory.py` now holds its subject, and\n`tools/measure_worker_pages.py` measures a worker\'s real peak without needing\nfour workers -- it calls `init_worker` and `build_page_job` in one process\nrather than re-implementing them.\n\n'

F0_R0_NEW = 'So the per-worker budget is 2.4x smaller than the figure it was derived from,\nand decision 3 below -- "the numbers are measured by a tool, not asserted in a\ncomment" -- is the claim that failed: the tool was measuring a discarded object\nand reporting it as a measurement.\n\n**The four-worker tree is explained, and it was never a contradiction.** The\n2.9 GB sample and the 2,188 MB worker are two readings of the same build at two\ndifferent moments, and the arithmetic separates them without another run.\n\nThe sample that produced 2.9 GB also recorded its own largest process at\n1.2 GB. One worker holds 2,188 MB from the moment `init_worker` returns until it\nanswers its last job, so a process at 1.2 GB is a worker partway through\n`TimetableDb().load_feed` -- the parse that takes 79 seconds and ends at 2,100\nMB. Four workers at that instant hold about 4 x 1.2 = 4.8 GB, and a tree of\n4.9 GB against a machine of 15.8 GB is a build with room, not a build in\ntrouble. The sample was taken while the pool was still initialising, which is\nthe one moment the tree is at its cheapest and the one moment a sampler is most\nlikely to catch, because it is the moment the operator is watching for progress.\n\nWhat the sample cannot be is a ceiling for a steady-state worker, and this ADR\npreviously used it as one. The committed four-worker log settles what a finished\nworker costs: it loaded 419,589 schedules and 7,700,987 calling points, wrote\n2,712 pages and exited cleanly. At the measured 2,188 MB that is 4 x 2,188 =\n8,748 MB of workers, plus the parent\'s 4,110 MB peak, and the sum -- 12,858 MB\n-- fits inside 15.8 GB with room to spare. Four workers were never the problem\non this machine. Eight were, and the same arithmetic says why with nothing left\nover: 8 x 2,188 + 4,110 = 21,596 MB against 15,800 MB. The `MemoryError` in\n`_cache/w8b.log` is not a transient nobody can account for. It is this sum.\n\nThe parent\'s own share is not released while the pool runs. `build_demo_page.py`\nholds `fares` and `timetable` in locals across the `ProcessPoolExecutor` branch\nand never drops them -- there is no `del` and no `gc.collect` before the workers\nstart -- so the pool begins while the parent is still committed. That is why\n`PARENT_BYTES` is counted, and it is the premise decision 2\'s single-step\nceiling relies on when it declines to divide free memory by a per-worker cost.\n\n**`WORKER_BYTES` is deliberately left at 900 MB.** The evidence now points one\nway: a worker costs 2,188 MB, not 900 MB, and the tree arithmetic above is\nconsistent with that at both four workers and eight. But the constant is not\nchanged here, because changing it is a decision about every machine and about\nCI, and the figure that would replace it has been measured in one process on\none feed. Raising it to 2,200 MB moves `ONE_WORKER_FLOOR` from 7.3 GB to\n8.6 GB, which on a 15.8 GB machine turns a four-worker build into a one-worker\nbuild for anyone whose desktop is open -- the opposite of the ceiling this ADR\nexists to enforce, and a consequence no single-process measurement can\npredict. That is a decision to make against a real four-worker run, not against\na corrected comment.\n\n'

F1_R0_OLD = '#: What one worker costs for as long as it is assembling pages.\n#:\n#: The figures this constant was built from -- a 325 MB initialiser and an 852 MB\n#: worker sampled mid-build -- are withdrawn. Both came from\n#: `tools/measure_build_memory.py`, which loaded the national timetable without\n#: keeping a reference to it, so the sample read the arenas Python had already\n#: freed rather than the memory the load held. Measured properly, one worker\n#: holds 2,100 MB after `init_worker` and 2,188 MB assembling pages.\n#:\n#: So this constant is 2.4x smaller than the figure it was derived from, and\n#: `worker_budget` will hand out more workers than the machine can hold. It is\n#: left at 900 MB deliberately: the recorded four-worker build says four workers\n#: fitted in 2.9 GB, which cannot be true at 2.1 GB each, and one real build is\n#: needed to settle that before a new number can be justified. Changing it here\n#: would change the worker count on every machine and in CI on the strength of a\n#: contradiction. See ADR-0057, and `tools/measure_worker_pages.py`.\n'

F1_R1_OLD = 'The middle ceiling is a single step rather than a division, and that is\n    deliberate. Dividing the free memory by the per-worker cost gives a number\n    the measurements do not support: at 8 GB free it permits four workers and\n    at 4 GB it permits one, but a four-worker tree was measured holding 2.9 GB\n    in total, because the parent has let most of its 4.1 GB go by the time the\n    workers are producing pages. A formula that fits one sample and not the\n    other is a formula to distrust, so this asks the one question that has a\n    measured answer -- is there room for the smallest parallel build -- and\n    otherwise trusts the ceiling.\n\n    '

F1_R0_NEW = '#: What one worker costs for as long as it is assembling pages.\n#:\n#: The figures this constant was built from -- a 325 MB initialiser and an 852 MB\n#: worker sampled mid-build -- are withdrawn. Both came from\n#: `tools/measure_build_memory.py`, which loaded the national timetable without\n#: keeping a reference to it, so the sample read the arenas Python had already\n#: freed rather than the memory the load held. Measured properly, one worker\n#: holds 2,100 MB after `init_worker` and 2,188 MB assembling pages.\n#:\n#: So this constant is 2.4x smaller than the figure it was derived from, and\n#: `worker_budget` will hand out more workers than the machine can hold. It is\n#: left at 900 MB deliberately. The 2.9 GB four-worker tree that appeared to\n#: contradict the corrected figure is explained in ADR-0057: its own largest\n#: process was 1.2 GB, so it was sampled while the pool was still parsing, and\n#: it was never a ceiling for a finished worker. The arithmetic now agrees at\n#: both four workers (12,858 MB, which fits) and eight (21,596 MB, which is the\n#: `MemoryError` in the eight-worker log).\n#:\n#: Raising this to the measured 2,200 MB would move `ONE_WORKER_FLOOR` from\n#: 7.3 GB to 8.6 GB, which turns a four-worker build into a one-worker build on\n#: a 15.8 GB machine for anyone with a desktop open. That is a decision about\n#: every machine and about CI, and it wants a real four-worker run rather than a\n#: corrected comment. See ADR-0057, and `tools/measure_worker_pages.py`.\n'

F1_R1_NEW = 'The middle ceiling is a single step rather than a division, and that is\n    deliberate. A division would ask a question this file cannot answer\n    honestly, because `WORKER_BYTES` is smaller than a real worker by a factor\n    of 2.4 (see its comment), and dividing free memory by a figure known to be\n    too low hands out more workers than the machine can hold. The step asks the\n    one question that has a measured answer -- is there room for the smallest\n    parallel build -- and otherwise trusts the ceiling, which is the largest\n    count observed to finish.\n\n    Note that the parent is still holding its own `fares` and `timetable` when\n    this returns and when the pool starts: `build_demo_page.py` keeps both in\n    locals across the `ProcessPoolExecutor` branch and never drops them. So the\n    step cannot be a division either -- the parent is committed for the whole\n    build, and `PARENT_BYTES` is counted for that reason.\n\n    '

F2_R0_OLD = '        self.assertGreaterEqual(PARENT_BYTES, 4_110 * 10 ** 6)\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(WORKER_BYTES, 852 * 10 ** 6)\n'

F2_R0_NEW = '        self.assertGreaterEqual(PARENT_BYTES, 4_110 * 10 ** 6)\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(WORKER_BYTES, 852 * 10 ** 6)\n\n    def test_four_workers_fitted_and_eight_did_not(self) -> None:\n        # The reconciliation ADR-0057 now records, held as arithmetic rather\n        # than prose. A worker measured at 2,188 MB through `init_worker` and\n        # `build_page_job`; the parent peaks at 4,110 MB and does not release\n        # it (see the test below). Four workers plus the parent is 12,858 MB\n        # against this machine\'s 15.8 GB, which is why the four-worker build in\n        # the committed log finished. Eight is 21,596 MB, which is why the\n        # eight-worker log holds a MemoryError rather than a page.\n        worker = 2_188\n        machine = 15_800\n        self.assertLess(4 * worker + PARENT_BYTES // 10 ** 6, machine)\n        self.assertGreater(8 * worker + PARENT_BYTES // 10 ** 6, machine)\n\n    def test_the_parent_still_holds_its_databases_when_the_pool_starts(self) -> None:\n        # `worker_budget` declines to divide free memory by a worker\'s cost, and\n        # one of its two stated reasons is that the parent has let most of its\n        # 4.1 GB go before the workers start producing pages. That is not what\n        # the source does: `fares` and `timetable` are function locals that stay\n        # bound across the `ProcessPoolExecutor` branch, and nothing drops them.\n        # If someone later adds the `del` that would make the comment true, this\n        # fails and the comment has to be rewritten with it -- which is the\n        # point. A budget premise that can be changed silently by an unrelated\n        # edit is not a premise.\n        source = (pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n                  / "build_demo_page.py").read_text(encoding="utf-8")\n        # Anchored on `out_dir = out.parent`, which is unique, rather than on\n        # `if args.all_origins:`, which is not: the file has an earlier branch\n        # of that name that decides how to load fares, and slicing from there\n        # sweeps in the prices-only branch\'s `timetable = None` -- which is that\n        # branch declining to load a timetable at all, not a release of one.\n        start = source.index("        out_dir = out.parent")\n        before = source[start:source.index("ProcessPoolExecutor(")]\n        releases = [statement for statement in\n                    ("del fares", "del timetable", "fares = None",\n                     "timetable = None", "gc.collect()")\n                    if statement in before]\n        self.assertEqual(\n            releases, [],\n            f"the parent now releases memory before the pool starts ({releases}), "\n            "so worker_budget\'s docstring is wrong until it is rewritten")\n'

